DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ggeettllooggiinn()  returns  a  pointer  to a string containing the name of the
       user logged in on the controlling terminal of the process,  or  a  null
       pointer if this information cannot be determined.  The string is stati‐
       cally  allocated  and  might be overwritten on subsequent calls to this
       function or to ccuusseerriidd().

       ggeettllooggiinn__rr() returns this same username in the array _b_u_f of  size  _b_u_f_‐
       _s_i_z_e.

       ccuusseerriidd()  returns  a pointer to a string containing a username associ‐
       ated with the effective user ID of the process.  If  _s_t_r_i_n_g  is  not  a
       null  pointer,  it  should be an array that can hold at least LL__ccuusseerriidd
       characters; the string is returned in this array.  Otherwise, a pointer
       to a string in a static area is returned.  This  string  is  statically
       allocated and might be overwritten on subsequent calls to this function
       or to ggeettllooggiinn().

       The  macro  LL__ccuusseerriidd is an integer constant that indicates how long an
       array you might need to store a username.   LL__ccuusseerriidd  is  declared  in
       _<_s_t_d_i_o_._h_>.

       These  functions  let  your program identify positively the user who is
       running (ccuusseerriidd()) or the user who  logged  in  this  session  (ggeettlloo‐‐
       ggiinn()).  (These can differ when set‐user‐ID programs are involved.)

       For  most  purposes,  it is more useful to use the environment variable
       LLOOGGNNAAMMEE to find out who the user is.  This is more  flexible  precisely
       because the user can set LLOOGGNNAAMMEE arbitrarily.

